  "description": "Convert continuous angular input measurements into a 3 x N feature array comprising sine, cosine, and tangent coordinates.\n\nDecomposing angles into sine and cosine coordinates preserves topological continuity across periodic 2pi phase boundaries.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = \\begin{bmatrix} \\sin(\\theta) \\\\ \\cos(\\theta) \\\\ \\tan(\\theta) \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Compute element-wise sine across angular inputs\n2. Compute element-wise cosine across angular inputs\n3. Compute element-wise tangent and stack along axis 0 into a (3, N) matrix\n\n### Returns\nNested list of shape (3, N) containing trigonometric feature rows.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Angle Features\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nIn computational mathematics and machine learning, robust algorithmic routines ensure calculations are both mathematically sound and computationally efficient. **Angle Features** implements a core computational building block.\n\nDecomposing angles into sine and cosine coordinates preserves topological continuity across periodic 2pi phase boundaries. In this problem, the goal is to: convert continuous angular input measurements into a 3 x n feature array comprising sine, cosine, and tangent coordinates.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = \\begin{bmatrix} \\sin(\\theta) \\\\ \\cos(\\theta) \\\\ \\tan(\\theta) \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Compute element-wise sine across angular inputs\n2. **Phase 2**: Compute element-wise cosine across angular inputs\n3. **Phase 3**: Compute element-wise tangent and stack along axis 0 into a (3, N) matrix\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Algorithmic Complexity**: Designing routines with optimal time and space complexity ensures they scale gracefully to large datasets.\n* **Numerical Accuracy**: Safeguarding calculations against floating-point underflow or division by zero ensures consistent results.\n* **Vectorization**: Implementing operations with contiguous array buffers avoids interpreter overhead.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: angles = [0.0, 0.785398, 1.570796]\n\nOutput: [[0.0, 0.707107, 1.0], [1.0, 0.707107, 0.0], [0.0, 1.0, 3060023.306953]]\n\nExplanation: Evaluated numpy_angle_features under standard test conditions."
  ],
  "requirements": "* Function must be named `numpy_angle_features`\n* Return value must match the exact expected structure and types\n* Handle edge cases cleanly and maintain numerical stability\n* Must run efficiently without redundant memory allocations",
